// Module 04, Exercise 04: Self-Referencing Structs
//
// In C, a struct can contain a pointer to itself.
// This is how linked lists, trees, and other data structures work.
//
// In crunch-c, you use pine[Self] to reference the current floorplan.

// Define a linked list node.
floorplan Node {
    value: i32
    next: pine[Node]
}

// Create three nodes and link them.
n1 = garrison(sizeof(Node))
n1.poke(Node.value, 10)
n1.poke(Node.next, none)  // no next node yet

n2 = garrison(sizeof(Node))
n2.poke(Node.value, 20)
n2.poke(Node.next, none)

n3 = garrison(sizeof(Node))
n3.poke(Node.value, 30)
n3.poke(Node.next, none)

// Link them: n1 -> n2 -> n3
n1.poke(Node.next, n2)
n2.poke(Node.next, n3)

// Traverse the list.
current = n1.peek(pine[Node])
while current != none {
    val = current.peek(Node.value)
    print(f"node value: {val}")
    current = current.peek(Node.next)
}

// YOUR TASK:
// 1. Run this file. Does it print 10, 20, 30?
// 2. Add a fourth node with value 40.
// 3. Write a function that counts the nodes in the list.
// 4. What happens if you forget to link a node?

// BONUS: Use bathysphere to inspect the raw memory
// of a node. Can you spot the value and the pointer?
